          <p class="section-intro">For the code you need to ship, the question you need to answer,
            and the routine you would rather not repeat.</p>
          <p class="workflow-note">Illustrative workflows — not live agent runs.</p>
          <div class="workflow-explorer" data-workflow-explorer>
            <nav class="workflow-tabs" aria-label="Choose a workflow">
              <a id="tab-build" href="#workflow-build" data-workflow="build"><span>01</span> Build <span class="tab-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
              <a id="tab-research" href="#workflow-research" data-workflow="research"><span>02</span> Research <span class="tab-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
              <a id="tab-automate" href="#workflow-automate" data-workflow="automate"><span>03</span> Automate <span class="tab-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
            </nav>
            <div class="workflow-panels">
              <article class="workflow-panel" id="workflow-build" data-panel="build">
                <div class="workflow-content"><span class="section-label">Your codebase. A second perspective.</span><h3>From stuck to shipped.</h3><p>Keep repository instructions in a skill, let a specialist investigate the failure, and run the test suite as a background job. Bring the findings back to the same conversation before deciding what to change.</p>
                  <p class="workflow-recipe"><span>BUILDING BLOCKS</span>Skills · subagents · background jobs · run evidence</p><p class="workflow-gain">Less handoff overhead: repository conventions, findings, and test results stay in one place instead of being copied between chats.</p>
                  <a class="workflow-link" href={`${DOCS_URL}getting-started/quickstart/`}>Start with a project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
                </div>
              </article>
              <article class="workflow-panel" id="workflow-research" data-panel="research">
                <div class="workflow-content"><span class="section-label">Your sources. A clearer picture.</span><h3>Connect the dots.</h3><p>Connect search and source material through tools or MCP. Give subagents separate questions to investigate, then compare their evidence in one conversation. Optional memory can retain useful context for the next session.</p>
                  <p class="workflow-recipe"><span>BUILDING BLOCKS</span>Tools & MCP · subagents · optional memory</p><p class="workflow-gain">Less repeated setup: return to the same brief, challenge the synthesis, and refine it without reintroducing every source.</p>
                  <a class="workflow-link" href={`${DOCS_URL}memory/`}>Explore optional memory <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
                </div>
              </article>
              <article class="workflow-panel" id="workflow-automate" data-panel="automate">
                <div class="workflow-content"><span class="section-label">Your routine. Less repetition.</span><h3>Find your rhythm.</h3><p>Configure a cron trigger, the sources to check, and a channel for the result. The same agent can prepare a recurring digest and answer your follow-up questions with its configured context.</p>
                  <p class="workflow-recipe"><span>BUILDING BLOCKS</span>Schedules · channels · tools · run evidence</p><p class="workflow-gain">Less checking by hand: information comes to you on a schedule, with a conversation ready when something needs attention.</p>
                  <a class="workflow-link" href={`${DOCS_URL}channels/cron/`}>Explore scheduled work <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
